-- Sale of Goods Act 1979 (Singapore).  CASES THAT ASSERT FINDINGS recorded in NOTES.md ("What the text turns out to say" and
-- "What the main binary does not accept") and not asserted by the case file of a Part.
-- Expected values are written from the text (or, for the binary, from the brief's rule) beside each assertion, before running.

IMPORT prelude
IMPORT daydate
IMPORT `soga-nouns`
IMPORT `soga-part7-supplementary`
IMPORT `soga-interpretation`
IMPORT `soga-schedule`
IMPORT `soga-part1-application`

-- Finding: paragraphs 2 to 4, 6, 10 and 13 of the Schedule have no floor of their own ("made before ..."), so each gate
-- answers JUST TRUE for a contract made before 1 January 1894; only paragraph 1(3) and s 1(1) take such a contract out, and
-- s 1(2) gathers that.
#ASSERT `s 11(5) paragraph 2 of the Schedule applies in relation to the contract` (Date 31 12 1893) EQUALS JUST TRUE
#ASSERT `s 12(6) paragraph 3 of the Schedule applies in relation to the contract` (Date 31 12 1893) EQUALS JUST TRUE
#ASSERT `s 14(6) paragraph 6 of the Schedule applies in relation to the contract` (Date 31 12 1893) EQUALS JUST TRUE
#ASSERT `sch para 1(3) the Schedule affects the contract` (Date 31 12 1893) EQUALS JUST FALSE
#ASSERT `s 1(2) this Act applies to the contract subject to the modification of the section mentioned in the Schedule` (Date 31 12 1893) `section 12` EQUALS JUST FALSE

-- Finding: the Legislative History says the Act was applied to Singapore on 12 November 1993; that note is "not part of the
-- Act".  Section 1(1) alone governs the dates: a contract made on 11 November 1993, or on 1 January 1894, is within the Act.
#ASSERT `s 1(1) this Act applies to the contract of sale of goods` (Date 11 11 1993) EQUALS JUST TRUE
#ASSERT `s 1(1) this Act applies to the contract of sale of goods` (Date 12 11 1993) EQUALS JUST TRUE
#ASSERT `s 1(1) this Act applies to the contract of sale of goods` (Date 1 1 1894) EQUALS JUST TRUE

-- Finding about the binary: Date takes any day and month number and rolls the result forward without an error.
-- 31 February 2025 is 3 March 2025 (31 - 28 = 3), and month 14 of 2025 is February 2026.
#ASSERT DATE_DAY (Date 31 2 2025) EQUALS 3
#ASSERT DATE_MONTH (Date 31 2 2025) EQUALS 3
#ASSERT DATE_MONTH (Date 15 14 2025) EQUALS 2
#ASSERT DATE_YEAR (Date 15 14 2025) EQUALS 2026

-- Finding about the binary: on main (1.6.109 and 1.6.108) an unbracketed NOT covers everything to its right, so
-- `NOT a OR b` reads as NOT (a OR b) and `NOT a AND b` as NOT (a AND b), not as (NOT a) OR b or (NOT a) AND b.  The run
-- exits 0 and prints no warning: a wrong answer, not an error.  Each probe below is a function so that the inputs, not
-- the parser's reading of a literal, decide the result.
GIVEN a IS A BOOLEAN
      b IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `probe: NOT a OR b, unbracketed` IS NOT a OR b

GIVEN a IS A BOOLEAN
      b IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `probe: NOT a OR b, bracketed` IS (NOT a) OR b

GIVEN a IS A BOOLEAN
      b IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `probe: NOT a AND b, unbracketed` IS NOT a AND b

GIVEN a IS A BOOLEAN
      b IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `probe: NOT a AND b, bracketed` IS (NOT a) AND b

#ASSERT `probe: NOT a OR b, unbracketed` TRUE TRUE EQUALS FALSE -- NOT (TRUE OR TRUE)
#ASSERT `probe: NOT a OR b, bracketed` TRUE TRUE EQUALS TRUE -- (NOT TRUE) OR TRUE
#ASSERT `probe: NOT a AND b, unbracketed` TRUE FALSE EQUALS TRUE -- NOT (TRUE AND FALSE)
#ASSERT `probe: NOT a AND b, bracketed` TRUE FALSE EQUALS FALSE -- (NOT TRUE) AND FALSE

-- assertions written: 16
